WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:07.940
(music)

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:13.680
Hi. I'm John Mitchell. I'm a professor of
Computer Science at Stanford University.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:18.900
I got a bachelor's degree in Math at
Stanford and I worked for a couple of

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:24.020
years, and I went and, ah, got a graduate
degree in Computer Science at MIT. When I

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:29.040
finished my PhD, I went to go work in an
industrial research lab, which was really

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.780
great in some ways, it was the ideal job.
I went in the morning, I had the whole

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.400
day to do whatever I want, but sort of after awhile I felt there was something a

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:43.660
little bit repetitive about it. And when
I came out to Stanford it to look at a

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:47.560
teaching job, I thought this would really
be much better. I think there are many,

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:51.720
many interesting things about
cybersecurity. Ah, trying to figure out the

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:56.280
difference between what people want from
technology and the things that we really

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.900
don't want, or we consider malicious. It's also really interesting to see how

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:04.400
people's attitudes change over time
about what they want and what they don't

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:09.720
want, what they find uncomfortable in
different ways. Ah, so we have students working

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:16.060
together on web security. Ah, some things
having to do with mobility. And we've

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:21.280
been very interested in the last few
years in privacy, web tracking, ah, things

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:24.920
that have to do with policy. But, but
generally speaking, how do we build

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:29.100
technology that's responsive to the
things that people want to do, and

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:33.900
protects against, ah, malicious things and
give some assurance about privacy and data.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.000
(music)

